NilBBS - a multi-node telnet BBS for the Amiga
===============================================

Needs: AmigaOS 3.1 or newer, a 68020 or better, a TCP/IP stack (Roadshow,
Miami or AmiTCP) and the standard Installer (AmigaOS 3.x has it).

INSTALLING
  Double-click Install_NilBBS.  It asks:
    - where to put the BBS drawer
    - the name of your BBS
    - your sysop handle and password (this account gets full sysop rights)
    - the telnet port (23) and how many callers at once (nodes)
    - whether to start NilBBS when the Amiga boots

  Then start your TCP/IP stack, double-click "Start NilBBS" in the BBS drawer
  and telnet in.  Log in with the sysop handle you chose.

UPDATING
  Run Install_NilBBS again and point it at the same place.  It replaces the
  programs only - your configuration, users, messages, files and game saves
  stay as they are.

WHAT'S IN THIS DRAWER
  Install_NilBBS    the installer
  BBS/              the BBS: programs and a default configuration

DOORS
  BBS/Doors holds four sample doors - one of each kind NilBBS runs - already on
  the doors menu (or ready to switch on) in BBS:Config/Doors.cfg:
    Guess     an AmigaDOS CLI program (type = cli) - its source is a template
    Hello     a CNet-style ARexx script (type = cnetrexx)
    CNTest    a CNet C PFile (type = cnetc)
    XIMTest   an AmiExpress XIM door (type = xim; needs AEDoor.library)
  Copy a section, change the name and command, and your door is on the menu -
  or use BBSConfig's Doors page.  CNet PFiles and AmiExpress doors run as they are.
  Door games come as their own packages, each with an installer that puts the game
  in BBS:PFiles and adds its Doors.cfg section (and works for CNet too).

IN THE BBS DRAWER AFTER INSTALLING
  Start NilBBS / Stop NilBBS   start and stop the BBS
  BBSConfig                    every setting, users, menus, doors, areas
  BBSControl                   the live sysop console: who's on, bans, one-liners
                               - and where callers' pages pop up, with a chat window
  BBSSchedule                  scheduled tasks: nightly maintenance, door maintenance,
                               mail, backups - add, edit, remove, run now
  NilTerm                      an ANSI telnet client (BBSControl's Logon button logs
                               on to a node with it): IBM PC screen, dialing
                               directory, scrollback, capture, ZMODEM/YMODEM/XMODEM.
                               Help shows its keys.  It also comes as a package of
                               its own (NilTerm.lha).  Font: IBM VGA 8x16
                               by VileR (int10h.org), CC BY-SA 4.0
  BBSCtl                       the same from a Shell (BBSCtl WHO, BBSCtl BANS ...)
  Run Maintenance              the nightly clean-up, by hand
  Local Logon                  log in on this Amiga without telnet

SAFETY
  Anyone who tries the handles admin, root or administrator is banned for
  good, and repeated bad passwords or connection floods ban an address for a
  while.  Put your own network on an "allow" line in BBS:Config/IPFilter.cfg.
  The Remote Shell door is switched off; see BBS:Config/Doors.cfg if you want it.

INSTALLING BY HAND
  Copy the BBS drawer where you want it, then from a Shell:
      CD <where you put it>/BBS
      Execute Setup
  Telnet in and sign up as NEW - the first account on a fresh BBS becomes
  the sysop.
  To have BBSControl open every time Workbench starts, copy WBStartup/BBSControl
  and its icon into SYS:WBStartup and set the icon's Default Tool to your
  BBS/BBSControl (Workbench's Icon > Information).
